  title="Water Stain on the Ceiling: How to Tell Where It Is Coming From"
  description="Roof, plumbing, or air conditioning? Three questions narrow a ceiling water stain down fast, plus what to do in the first hour and why drying comes first."
>
<p>A ceiling stain is almost never directly under the thing that caused it. Water runs along framing, decking, and pipes before it finds a seam in the drywall and drops, so the brown ring you are looking at can be several feet from the actual source. There are four usual suspects: the roof and its flashings, a supply or drain line above, the air conditioning system's condensate, and the bathroom overhead. You can narrow it down in about ten minutes with three questions, before anybody opens a ceiling.</p>

<h2>The three questions that sort it out</h2>

<p><strong>Does it only show up when it rains?</strong> That points at the roof envelope. Flashing around a chimney, a vent pipe boot, a valley, a skylight, a wall intersection, or a lifted shingle. Rain driven by wind can also push water in sideways at a soffit or a gable vent, which produces a stain near an exterior wall that appears only in storms from one direction.</p>

<p><strong>Does it only show up when somebody uses water upstairs?</strong> That points at plumbing. A drain or a fixture that leaks only in use, which is the most common version of this problem, and the easiest one to confirm. Run the tub for five minutes, then the shower, then flush, then run the sink, waiting between each one, and watch the stain. Whichever fixture makes it wet is your answer.</p>

<p><strong>Does it show up on hot humid days with the air conditioning running, and no rain?</strong> That is the condensate system almost every time. A clogged drain line, an overflowing secondary pan in the attic, or a duct or plenum sweating because its insulation failed.</p>

<p>If the stain grows continuously and does not care about rain, showers, or weather, assume a pressurized supply line and shut the water off at the main until somebody finds it. A supply leak is the one version of this that gets worse by the hour.</p>

<h2>Reading the location</h2>

<p><strong>Directly under a bathroom.</strong> The usual order is the toilet's wax ring, then the tub or shower drain connection, then failed caulk or grout at a tile surround letting water behind the wall. A toilet flange leak often comes with a floor that feels soft near the base of the toilet upstairs.</p>

<p><strong>Under an attic, with no bathroom above.</strong> Roof or HVAC. In Middle Tennessee, an attic air handler with a clogged condensate line is one of the most common causes of a summer ceiling stain in a single story house.</p>

<p><strong>Around a ceiling light, a fan, or a bath fan housing.</strong> Those fixtures are holes in the drywall, so they are where water exits. Turn the breaker off for that circuit before touching anything or getting on a ladder near it. A fixture with water in it is an electrical problem on top of a water problem.</p>

<p><strong>Along a straight line or a drywall seam.</strong> Water traveled and found the joint. The source can be well off to one side.</p>

<p><strong>At the perimeter, where the ceiling meets an exterior wall.</strong> Gutters and the roof edge. Overflowing or blocked gutters push water back under the shingle edge, and ice at the eave does the same thing in a cold snap. Clearing the gutters is often the whole repair.</p>

<h2>The roof path</h2>

<p>Roofs rarely leak in the open field of the shingles. They leak at interruptions: chimneys, plumbing vent boots whose rubber collars crack after years of sun, step flashing where a roof meets a wall, valleys where two planes drain together, skylights, and satellite mount holes nobody ever sealed. Nail pops and wind lifted shingles are the next tier.</p>

<p>Age matters because the materials around those interruptions age faster than the shingles. If the roof is in the back half of its life, a single stain is worth a look at the whole thing rather than one patch. Our guide to <a href="/articles/how-to-assess-roof-age-and-condition/">assessing roof age and condition</a> walks through how to tell where yours stands.</p>

<h2>The plumbing path</h2>

<p>Supply leaks are constant and growing. Drain leaks are intermittent and tied to use. That single distinction saves most of the guesswork.</p>

<p>The specific culprits, in the order we find them: toilet wax ring or a loose flange, tub or shower drain shoe and overflow gasket, a failed shower pan or cracked tile grout, a supply line to a toilet or sink, a washing machine box or hose, and an angle stop that weeps slowly behind a vanity. The pattern for an overflow gasket is distinctive. It only leaks when the water level reaches the overflow plate, which means a bath leaks and a shower does not. <a href="/services/plumbing/">Our plumbing work</a> starts with isolating which one it is rather than opening the ceiling and hoping.</p>

<h2>The air conditioning path</h2>

<p>Every air conditioner pulls humidity out of your air and has to send that water somewhere. In our summers that is gallons a day.</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Clogged condensate drain.</strong> Biological growth closes the line, the primary pan fills, and it overflows. Peak season, hottest weeks, no rain.</li>
<li><strong>Secondary pan with no working float switch.</strong> The pan under an attic air handler is the backup, and it only helps if the float switch that shuts the system off is installed and wired. Many are not.</li>
<li><strong>Sweating ducts and plenums.</strong> Insulation separates or was never sealed, humid attic air hits a cold metal surface, and it rains inside your attic. The stain appears along the duct run rather than under a fixture.</li>
<li><strong>A frozen coil that thaws.</strong> Low airflow from a dirty filter or a refrigerant problem ices the coil, then it melts past the pan all at once.</li>
</ul>

<p>If the stain shows up in July and vanishes in October, stop looking at the roof. <a href="/services/hvac/">The HVAC side</a> is where the answer is.</p>

<h2>What to do in the next hour</h2>

<ol>
<li><strong>Photograph it with the date.</strong> You will want the before picture whether this becomes an insurance claim, a tenant conversation, or a repair invoice.</li>
<li><strong>Outline the edge in pencil.</strong> It is the simplest test there is. If the stain grows past your line over the next day, it is active. If it does not, you may be looking at an old stain from a leak that was already fixed.</li>
<li><strong>Put a container under it</strong> and move anything that matters out from under the area.</li>
<li><strong>Kill the breaker</strong> to any light or fan in the wet zone.</li>
<li><strong>If it is growing fast, shut off the water,</strong> either to the fixture above or at the main.</li>
<li><strong>Do not paint it yet.</strong> Paint over an active leak and you will be repainting. Paint over a wet cavity and you will be cutting it out later.</li>
</ol>

<h2>When the ceiling has to come open</h2>

<p>Sometimes the only way to be sure is to look. A single small inspection cut in the right place, chosen after the questions above, costs far less than three repaints chasing a stain that keeps coming back. When we open one, we open it where the water is most likely entering, verify the source while the water is running, and dry the cavity before anything gets patched.</p>

<p>Drying first is the part people skip. Insulation holds water, drywall patched over a damp cavity stains again from behind, and paint alone will not seal a tannin stain anyway. The sequence that works is find the source, fix the source, dry it out, prime with a stain blocking primer, then patch and paint. Any other order gets you back on a ladder in a month.</p>

<p>If you have a stain on a ceiling in a house or a rental in Middle Tennessee and you would rather know than guess, <a href="/work-order/">send us the details and a photo</a>. We find the source first, fix it, repaint it, and send you pictures of the whole thing when it is done.</p>
